    sources: [{ id: "src_sacks_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Oliver Sacks (Q258662) -- verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-23): English label 'Oliver Sacks', description 'British neurologist and writer (1933-2015)', born 9 July 1933, died 30 August 2015, citizenship United Kingdom, Korean label '올리버 색스', English and Korean Wikipedia sitelinks present. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q258662" }, { id: "src_sacks_foundation", kind: "institution", title: "Oliver Sacks Foundation, 'Oliver Sacks Author | Biography, Life and Books by Neurologist' (oliversacks.com), read in full: trained at Oxford, residencies in San Francisco and at UCLA, moved to New York in 1965, consulting neurologist at Beth Abraham Hospital in the Bronx from 1966, professor at Columbia (2007-12, its first 'Columbia University Artist') and NYU School of Medicine (2012-15); book list (Awakenings, Migraine, The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat, An Anthropologist on Mars, Musicophilia and others); Lewis Thomas Prize 2002; the 1990 Awakenings film; his approach of looking at how each individual coped rather than fitting patients to a category; 'often credited' as a forerunner of the neurodiversity movement. CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE. Estate-run foundation page, so admiring and not independent; used for career facts and significance orientation, never as sole support for a trait row. Its passages on private life were not used.", url: "https://www.oliversacks.com/about-oliver-sacks/" }, { id: "src_sacks_ebsco", kind: "biography", title: "EBSCO Research Starters, 'Awakenings by Oliver Sacks' (ebsco.com), read in full: Sacks about a year out of residency in 1969; L-dopa given from spring 1969 to postencephalitic patients, many catatonic for decades; dramatic but often temporary recoveries, adverse reactions that forced dose reductions; the book's departure from the conventional case history (detailed notes, patients encouraged to keep journals, biographical accounts); twenty case histories, a 1987 edition with epilogue updating them; first book Migraine (1970); Einstein College of Medicine professor of clinical neurology and private practice; the book's setting based on Beth Abraham; reviews listed but not summarised. Reference overview, largely favourable.", url: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/literature-and-writing/awakenings-oliver-sacks" }, { id: "src_sacks_bmj", kind: "press", title: "Charlotte Allan, 'Awakenings' (BMJ 'Medical Classic' essay, 2 June 2007; PubMed Central), read in full: levodopa hailed as a 'miracle drug' in 1969, unpredictable effects even with slow titration; the patients Miriam H, Leonard L, Miron V and Rose R and how they responded; argues that Sacks treated patients as people and that his long-term relationship with them was crucial; psychological and environmental factors seemed to affect the drug's effect. Medical-journal essay by a junior doctor; it does not describe his methods or colleagues' reception.", url: "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1885331/" }, { id: "src_sacks_truthdig", kind: "press", title: "Truthdig (Kasia Anderson), 'Oliver Sacks, Neurological Innovator and \"Awakenings\" Author, Dies at 82' (31 August 2015), read in full: obituary round-up that quotes a Guardian tribute describing his clinical tales as literature (a musician with visual agnosia, Tourette's syndrome, deaf people who 'see' voices, an autistic boy with a gift for drawing) in the lineage of Freud's case histories and Luria's 'neurographies'; Variety's praise for explaining brain science to general readers; the Awakenings film's three Oscar nominations. Contemporaneous obituary material at second hand; no criticism and no institutional detail; its cause-of-death passage was not used.", url: "https://www.truthdig.com/articles/oliver-sacks-neurological-innovator-and-awakenings-author-dies-at-82/" }, { id: "src_sacks_nautilus", kind: "press", title: "Pria Anand, 'The Confabulations of Oliver Sacks' (Nautilus), read in full through the fetch tool: a neurologist's essay summarising Rachel Aviv's 15 December 2025 New Yorker article built on Sacks's journals and letters -- the 'caged panther' Rilke image attributed to the patient Leonard in Awakenings that Sacks's own letters suggest was his own, the patient 'Rebecca' in The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat whose reported recovery his journals contradict, Sacks calling the book 'fairy tales' and 'confabulations' in a letter to his brother, and the reading that this was confabulation rather than deliberate lying. Secondary summary (the New Yorker article itself was not opened); its passages on his hidden identity and psychological interpretation were not used. COUNTER-VOICE (factual).", url: "https://nautil.us/the-confabulations-of-oliver-sacks-1262447" }, { id: "src_sacks_horgan", kind: "press", title: "John Horgan, 'Oliver Sacks Fudged Facts. Does That Bother Me?' (Cross-Check column, johnhorgan.org), read in full: responds to the same New Yorker article -- Leonard portrayed as bookish and the Rilke panther quotation, the invented number-swapping scene in 'The Twins', Aviv's report that Sacks kept to facts in An Anthropologist on Mars (1995) and later books; Horgan defends the narrative, anti-reductionist method and notes Sacks never used case studies to prove a pet theory; critics in his postscript (George Johnson, Michael Brooks, Faye Flam) call embellished case histories dishonest, with Flam noting that a critical investigator would have tested the twins with non-prime numbers. Opinion column, partly admiring and partly critical. COUNTER-VOICE (factual). Treated with the Nautilus essay as ONE underlying perspective for the fabrication findings, since both rest on Aviv.", url: "https://johnhorgan.org/cross-check/oliver-sacks-fudged-facts-does-that-bother-me" }, { id: "src_sacks_mcginn", kind: "press", title: "Colin McGinn, 'Oliver Sacks' Fabrications' (colinmcginn.net blog post, after the December 2025 New Yorker article), read in full: says he raised the question of fabrication -- 'exaggeration, embellishment, gilding the lily' -- in his 1986 London Review of Books review of The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat; says Sacks never admitted the criticism was justified but did not deny it; says Musicophilia and On the Move lacked comparable inaccuracies. Independent critic with a personal stake in the matter (he says his own reputation was damaged); his claims about the 1986 review are used only as his own report. COUNTER-VOICE.", url: "https://colinmcginn.net/oliver-sacks-fabrications/" }, { id: "src_sacks_psychtimes", kind: "press", title: "H. Steven Moffic, 'In Memoriam Epilogue: The Psychiatrist Who Mistook Oliver Sacks for a Psychiatrist Confesses' (Psychiatric Times, 13 January 2026), read in full: a psychiatrist retracts his 2015 eulogy, reporting that he had credited Sacks with careful, humane evaluations of unusual patients and now thinks the literary presentation was beautiful but medically misleading after the New Yorker report on invented case material; mentions Sacks's own journal wording about 'lies, falsifications'. Opinion column by a colleague-observer; its speculation about Sacks's psychotherapy and psychology was not used. Retrospective critical voice.", url: "https://www.psychiatrictimes.com/view/in-memoriam-epilogue-the-psychiatrist-who-mistook-oliver-sacks-for-a-psychiatrist-confesses" }, { id: "src_sacks_commons", kind: "institution", title: "Wikimedia Commons file page '9.13.09OliverSacksByLuigiNovi (cropped).jpg' (and the parent file page), checked for the portrait's licence: photograph by Luigi Novi at the Brooklyn Book Festival, 13 September 2009, own work, CC BY 3.0 with a credit-line request; the crop is 780 x 1131. Not used for any scoring.", url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9.13.09OliverSacksByLuigiNovi_(cropped).jpg" }],
