      // [src_sacks_ebsco, src_sacks_foundation, src_sacks_truthdig, src_sacks_horgan | 1970-1985 | reference overview + foundation page + obituary tribute + opinion column] EBSCO says Sacks found conventional clinical case histories too limited, kept detailed notes, encouraged patients to keep journals and built biographical case histories (Awakenings, 1973); the Guardian tribute quoted by Truthdig places his tales in a lineage from Freud and Luria and calls them literature; Horgan describes a narrative, anti-reductionist method that put the human subject back at the centre of the case history and distinguishes him from Freud's use of cases to support a theory. A distinctive form that outside writers describe as his own, but most of the praise is retrospective, the foundation page is not independent, and the same method is the one critics say blurred fact and invention, so strong inference at a moderate confidence.
