      // [src_sacks_nautilus, src_sacks_horgan, src_sacks_mcginn, src_sacks_psychtimes | 1973-1985, reported 2025-2026 | commentary on a journalistic investigation + opinion column + 1986 reviewer + colleague column] The Nautilus essay and Horgan both report Aviv's finding that Sacks's journals and letters show invented or altered details in published case histories (the Rilke caged-panther image given to the patient Leonard, the patient 'Rebecca' in The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat, the number-swapping scene in 'The Twins'); Horgan notes Faye Flam's point that the twins were never tested with non-prime numbers; McGinn says he raised exaggeration and embellishment in his 1986 London Review of Books review; Moffic retracts his 2015 praise of the clinical evaluations. The fabrication claim rests on one underlying investigation (not opened directly) plus an independent 1986 critic, and the same sources say Sacks kept to the facts in An Anthropologist on Mars and later books, so a below-average rating on checking and fidelity to observation in his early case writing at a moderate confidence, risk impact, with confabulation rather than intent as the sources' own reading. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (lead review, before eligibility): re-based from 40 / 0.52 strong_inference to 44 / 0.44 inference (confidence and evidence type lowered; score moved toward neutral because the evidence is weaker, not stronger). The fabrication finding reaches this profile only through three commentaries on a journalistic investigation that was not opened directly, so the primary evidence is second-hand; the independent 1986 critique supports embellishment in general, not the specific findings.
      analytical_rigor: [44, 0.44, "i", "R"],
      // [src_sacks_foundation, src_sacks_ebsco, src_sacks_bmj | 1966-1987 | foundation page + reference overview + medical-journal essay] Sacks was consulting neurologist at Beth Abraham from 1966 and followed the postencephalitic patients through the 1969 L-dopa trial, its relapses and dose reductions into the 1987 edition of Awakenings, whose epilogue updates the twenty case histories; the BMJ essay argues that his long-term relationship with the patients was crucial for them and their families. A long, sustained clinical and documentary commitment is described by three sources, but they are favourable overviews and the span of his own presence is partly inferred from publication dates, so strong inference at a modest confidence.
      persistence: [62, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_sacks_foundation, src_sacks_ebsco, src_sacks_truthdig | 1966-2015 | foundation page + reference overview + obituary tribute] He practised and taught clinical neurology (Albert Einstein College of Medicine, Columbia, NYU) while writing for The New Yorker and the New York Review of Books; Columbia made him its first 'Columbia University Artist'; the Guardian tribute and Variety, quoted by Truthdig, describe him as writing clinical stories as literature and explaining brain science to general readers; EBSCO places him in a tradition of physician-authors. Sustained work across medicine and literature is recorded by the institution, a reference work and an obituary, and no source disputes it, so documented at a moderate confidence (the foundation page is not independent).
      cross_domain_range: [70, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // [src_sacks_ebsco, src_sacks_foundation, src_sacks_horgan, src_sacks_mcginn | 1973-1986 | reference overview + foundation page + opinion column + 1986 reviewer] EBSCO says he criticised the dry, impersonal style of medical journals and chose the biographical case history instead; the foundation page says he looked at how each individual coped rather than fitting patients into a diagnostic category; Horgan calls him an anti-theorist who insisted on seeing each patient as an individual; McGinn's account of his 1986 critique shows that the chosen form drew a sceptical reviewer. A self-chosen method that departed from the field's convention is documented by four sources, but the tenor is largely admiring and the description of his reasons comes mostly from retrospective summaries, so strong inference at a modest confidence, dual-edged because the same departure from convention is what critics say loosened fact-checking.
